I fixed the session-claim window. The trajectory no longer flags day-212-20260928T110922Z, which had landed two task commits but was reported as having none.

The bug was confirmed in the code. classify_session_claims used [own_stamp, next_stamp) as each session's window. But a session directory's stamp marks when the session ended: T110922Z has the same second as its wrap-up commit 2f597a87 (11:09:22Z), and its task commits came earlier, at 10:43 and 11:04. So every session's commits were being counted for the session after it.

What changed (scripts/extract_trajectory.py):
• The window is now `(previous stamp, own stamp]`. It includes the stamp itself because the wrap-up commit carries that exact second.
• The oldest session in the list has no predecessor, so its window has no start. It is now CLAIM_OPEN_WINDOW: it is neither counted as confirmed nor flagged. Before, the newest session was the one with the open window.
• **A second bug the task didn't mention:** the commit reader used committer time (`%ct`). The rebase at push time rewrites that to a few seconds *after* the directory stamp: the 20:26 session's commits all read 21:01:41Z against a stamp of 21:01:36Z. That would have pushed every session's commits out of its own window, so the reader now uses author time (%at).

Tests: two new self-tests go through classify_session_claims using real session-directory names:
• Today's layout: three end-stamped sessions with commits 25 minutes before each stamp. All three count as confirmed.
• A near-miss: a session whose only task commit is 60 s after its own stamp. It is still flagged CLAIM_NO_COMMITS.

The existing fixtures needed a predecessor session and commits before each stamp. I also ran a positive control in one command: with the window put back to the old direction, the new test day-212 shape: day-212-20260928T110922Z is corroborated by its own commits failed by name, and after restoring the file everything passed again.

Real run over the audit-log branch, same clone:

| | Flagged ("claimed success, no task commits") | Could NOT be checked |
|---|---|---|
| Before | 1 of 3 closed sessions | 5 |
| After | 0 of 3 closed sessions | 6 |

The extra unchecked session is correct: windows now start at the previous stamp, so one more window begins before the oldest commit this 50-commit clone has.

Checks: extract_trajectory.py --test passes, tests/harness_logic.sh passes (142/0), cargo build is clean and cargo test shows no failures. I didn't run cargo clippy; nothing in src/ changed.

The write-up, with the numbers above, is in ARCHITECTURE.md under the extract_trajectory.py entry. The final commit is ae6f9553 with the task title; 4221fc31 is a work-in-progress commit I made before running cargo. I renamed my local unpushed WIP commit (git commit --amend) so the final one carries the task title.
